  "metadata": {
    "title": "Anthropic scouts data center leases with Google backing : The Information",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "Anthropic is pursuing its first direct data center leases in the United States, signing more than a dozen preliminary agreements for facilities with combined capacity exceeding 1 gigawatt, according to The Information. The company is also discussing an arrangement in which Google would provide financial backing for the lease payments.\nThe plan would shift Anthropic's infrastructure strategy after years of securing computing capacity by renting chip servers from cloud providers such as Amazon Web Services and Google. Under the reported plan, Anthropic would lease and manage the facilities itself, with Google, which co-designs some of the server chips Anthropic could use, helping underwrite the obligations.\nA gigawatt-plus leasing pipeline puts Anthropic in the same capacity bracket as hyperscalers and major power consumers. For context, that is roughly the output of a large nuclear plant, enough to influence the same site selection, power procurement, and interconnection markets tracked by data center operators and energy-intensive industries.\nGoogle's deepening role\nGoogle has several roles in Anthropic's expansion: cloud provider, chip ecosystem partner, and strategic investor. Court documents cited by DatacenterDynamics showed Google held a 14% stake in Anthropic, with prior investments of around $2 billion in 2023 and another $1 billion in 2025. Alphabet has said it planned to invest up to $40 billion in the company.\nThe financial backing now under discussion would deepen that role. Bloomberg reported earlier this month that Google was already backstopping lease payments at five Anthropic-linked data center sites. Separately, reporting by the Financial Times indicated Google was planning to provide financial support, including possible construction loans, for a 2,800-acre Texas data center campus aimed at 500 MW initially, with potential expansion to 7.7 GW. That project alone could cost up to $5 billion in its first phase.\nCloud commitments continue\nThe direct leasing push would expand Anthropic's infrastructure plans while its cloud relationships continue. In April, Anthropic and Amazon announced an expanded agreement for up to 5 GW of new compute capacity, with Anthropic committing more than $100 billion over ten years to AWS technologies.\nAnthropic called AWS its primary training and cloud provider for mission-critical workloads and said it was running Claude on more than one million Trainium2 chips. Amazon said it was investing $5 billion in Anthropic, with up to an additional $20 billion in the future.\nIPO and valuation context\nAnthropic confidentially filed for a U.S. IPO earlier this month. It last raised funds in late May, securing $65 billion at a post-money valuation of $965 billion, putting it ahead of OpenAI.",
